                "section": "phy",
                "subject": "phy",
                "question": "Three identical capacitors, each of 6 μF, are connected so that their effective capacitance is 9 μF. How are they connected?",
                "options": [
                    "All in parallel",
                    "All in series",
                    "Two in parallel, then series with third",
                    "Two in series, then parallel with third"
                ],
                "marks": 2,
                "imageUrl": "https://www.google.com/imgres?q=Three%20identical%20capacitors%2C%20each%20of%206%20%CE%BCF%2C%20are%20connected%20so%20that%20their%20effective%20capacitance%20is%209%20%CE%BCF.%20How%20are%20they%20connected%3F%22%2C&imgurl=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.sarthaks.com%2F%3Fqa%3Dblob%26qa_blobid%3D16815458841627749701&imgrefurl=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.sarthaks.com%2F54213%2Fthree-identical-capacitors-c1-c2-and-c3-capacitance-each-are-connected-12v-battery-shown&docid=MKklwiz1VXFt7M&tbnid=6si6qiB5T04-VM&vet=12ahUKEwj5vIfiwYGXAxU9jeEIHbATBBIQnPAOegQIPhAA..i&w=196&h=126&hcb=2&ved=2ahUKEwj5vIfiwYGXAxU9jeEIHbATBBIQnPAOegQIPhAA",
                "paragraph": "For three identical capacitors, each having a capacitance of 6 μF, an effective capacitance of 9 μF can be obtained by connecting two capacitors in series and then connecting this series combination in parallel with the third capacitor. The equivalent capacitance of two 6 μF capacitors connected in series is 3 μF. This is calculated using the series capacitance formula, where the reciprocal of the equivalent capacitance is equal to the sum of the reciprocals of the individual capacitances. The 3 μF series combination is then connected in parallel with the remaining 6 μF capacitor. Therefore, the total effective capacitance is 3 μF plus 6 μF, which equals 9 μF. Hence, the correct arrangement is two capacitors in series, with their combination connected in parallel with the third capacitor."
            },
